Business Impact Analysis
A Business Impact Analysis evaluates the severity and operational impact of vulnerabilities identified on target host 192.168.1.165 (Metasploitable2). 
 
Executive Summary 
The target system represents an extreme operational risk to the organization. Multiple critical vulnerabilities including a built-in FTP backdoor, remote code execution via PHP CGI, unauthenticated SMB null sessions, and default system credentials allow unauthenticated attackers to obtain full administrative control within seconds. 
 
Threat Landscape & Vulnerability Profile
 
Critical Remote Code Execution (RCE) Vectors:
 
vsFTPd 2.3.4 Backdoor (Port 21): Contains a known backdoor trigger allowing direct execution to a root shell. Penetration testing confirmed immediate root access and full directory traversal (/root, /etc/shadow). 
 
PHP CGI Argument Injection (Port 80): Exploitation of CVE-2012-1823 via phpMyAdmin allowed an attacker to spawn a reverse shell as www-data. 
 
Weak Authentication & Credential Exposure:
 
Default Credentials (SSH/FTP): Valid login via msfadmin:msfadmin provided interactive SSH shell access. The user msfadmin possesses full sudo rights without restrictions (ALL). 
 
Anonymous Access: Port 21 permits anonymous:anonymous access. 
Password Hash Exposure: Root shell access directly compromised /etc/shadow, exposing password hashes for system users including root, postgres, sys, and msfadmin. 
 
 
Insecure Storage & File System Permissiveness:
 
Writable SMB Shares: SMB network shares /tmp and /msfadmin grant full READ/WRITE permissions. Automated file discovery revealed arbitrary webshell uploads (e.g., php-reverse-shell.php) stored within /opt and /tmp. 
 
SMB Null Sessions & Protocol Risks: SMBv1 is enabled with null authentication and message signing disabled (signing: False), exposing the network to NTLM relay attacks and user enumeration. 
 
